33 passing, typecheck clean, no casts remain.

Refactor complete.

## Naming evaluation (first priority)

Reviewed every name in the file. `basePremiumOf`/`insuranceValueOf` are well-paired, `matchDamagesToInsuredItems` and `settleClaim` state exactly what they do, and `isInsurable` reads as the question it answers. No renames warranted — the existing names already reveal intent.

One new name was needed: the extracted helper is `insurableTypeOf`, matching the `…Of(item)` shape of its two callers.

## Refactoring applied (Rule 3 — no duplication)

The duplication was conceptual, not textual: **"is this item type one the MHPCO insures?"** was answered twice, in two different ways. `basePremiumOf` asked it properly via the `isInsurable` type guard; `insuranceValueOf` assumed the answer with `item.type as InsurableItemType`. Same domain question, two representations — and one of them was a lie the type system had been told to accept.

Extracted the gate both lookups now pass through, in `/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-09-05_11-06-59_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.1.5-pure-split-cc_opus-5-no-thinking-2/src/claim-office.ts`:

```typescript
const insurableTypeOf = (item: Item): InsurableItemType => {
  if (!isInsurable(item.type)) {
    throw new Error(`The MHPCO does not insure a ${item.type}`);
  }
  return item.type;
};

const basePremiumOf = (item: Item): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[insurableTypeOf(item)];
const insuranceValueOf = (item: Item): number => INSURANCE_VALUES[insurableTypeOf(item)];
```

`basePremiumOf` also shed its braces and became a single expression, matching `insuranceValueOf`'s shape — the two now read as the parallel lookups they are.

## APP mass

| | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| `isInsurable` | 4 | 4 |
| gate / `basePremiumOf` | 19 | 19 (gate) + 4 (lookup) |
| `insuranceValueOf` | 4 | 4 |
| **Total** | **27** | **31** |

Mass rose by 4 — one extra invocation and binding for the added indirection. I took that trade deliberately: Rule 2 and Rule 3 both outrank APP, and the cast's zero mass was purchased by suppressing a real type error. The `as` was the cheapest possible expression of a fact that wasn't true.

## Behavioural note (not covered by a current test)

This closes a latent bug rather than only tidying. Previously `INSURANCE_VALUES["broomstick"]` returned `undefined`, so `openPolicy` computed `remainingCap: NaN` and every later claim silently compared against `NaN` — payouts of `NaN` with no error anywhere. That's the same failure mode the Green phase just fixed in `basePremiumOf`, still live on the claim path.

The existing unknown-item-type test (spec line 587) doesn't catch it: that scenario is quote-only, and `quotePremium` throws via `basePremiumOf` before `openPolicy` is ever reached. So the fix is currently unverified by any test. Worth an `it.todo` on a future cycle — an unknown item type reaching a *claim* — though I've left the test list untouched, as adding tests isn't the refactor phase's job.
